    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "The distance from Anna's to Mary's house is 16 kilometers along the shown road. The distance from Mary's to Nick's house is 20 kilometers. The distance from Nick's to John's house is 19 kilometers. How far is Anna's house from John's?",
    "image": "qimages/kangaroo/ca-2018-g0304/q24.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "15"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "16"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "18"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "19"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "20"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Use the crossing",
        "body": "All four roads meet at the crossing in the middle. Call the distances from the crossing to the houses $a$ (Anna), $m$ (Mary), $n$ (Nick) and $j$ (John). Then\n\n- $a + m = 16$\n- $m + n = 20$\n- $n + j = 19$\n\nAdd the first and the third: $a + m + n + j = 35$. Take away the middle one: $a + j = 35 - 20 = 15$ km.\n\n**Answer: (A) 15**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Think in road pieces",
        "body": "Cut the roads at the crossing into four pieces: Anna's, Mary's, Nick's and John's piece. Anna–Mary (16 km) plus Nick–John (19 km) uses each of the four pieces exactly once: 35 km. Mary–Nick (20 km) is just Mary's and Nick's pieces. What is left, $35 - 20 = 15$ km, is Anna's piece plus John's piece — the way from Anna to John.\n\n**Answer: (A) 15**"
      }
    ],
